// Go language package. Declarations here are visible at every output target
// that selects --lang go.

import . "sngl:macro"
import ir "sngl:internal/ir"
import http "sngl:remote/http"
import build "sngl:build"
import marks "sngl:internal/marks"
import shapes "sngl:ui/draw"

// The `go` node of a build directive: `output { go { bubbletea() } }`. Its
// props are the build options every Go target accepts, and its slot is the
// platforms built with them.
component go(
    // Go toolchain version emitted in generated go.mod files. Unset takes the
    // host's own go.mod line, and 1.23 where there is none.
    goVersion string,
    // Extra text appended verbatim to temp module go.mod files written by
    // platform test runners. Typical use: a `replace
    // git.duckfam.us/jonathan/sngl => /path/to/repo` directive so generated
    // code that imports SNGL runtime packages (pkg/go/i18n, …) resolves
    // locally instead of via the public proxy.
    goModExtra string,
    platforms ...component build.platform,
) build.language {}

// --- sngl:remote/http ---

// The Go transport. `native` rather than a `go:` import, so the request package
// is pulled in only by a build that actually fetches: an import at the top of
// this file is resolved by every program built for Go.
// Names the Go package-level identifier a declaration *is*. A thin spelling of
// #[foreign(..., native)] in the language it is about, which is where a Go path
// and a Go identifier belong — and which a package can now use on its own
// declarations, since a mark resolves through the scope like any other name.
//
// One argument is the identifier alone. That is all a struct *field* can say:
// a field has no package of its own, and what it records is the Go spelling of
// a name SNGL writes differently — `#[go.native("ID")] id int`. The two-argument
// form is everything else, where the path is what the call site has to import.
func native(path string, name string = "", flags list<NativeFlag> = []) ir.Macro

// Says a call to this function blocks, so a target that draws on one thread
// must not make the call there.
//
// Go has no way to suspend a function: a call runs on the goroutine that made
// it, and on fyne, gtk4 or bubbletea that goroutine is the one drawing the
// screen. The mark is what tells a target to run the call on a goroutine of its
// own and hand the answer back through whatever reaches its thread — `fyne.Do`,
// a GLib idle tick, a message into the Bubble Tea loop.
//
// It is not `#[foreign(..., async)]`. That flag says the declaration *returns a
// promise its caller awaits*, which is what the TypeScript importer reads off a
// `Promise<T>`; a Go function that blocks returns its value directly and has no
// promise, so the flag would be a false statement about the declaration. What
// the two do share is the propagation rule, and so the fact they both record: a
// function that calls one is one.
//
// Only a function carries it — a struct has no call to describe.
func async() ir.Macro

// What a Go declaration's shape says beyond its name.
//
// `fails` says the function returns `(T, error)` where the SNGL declaration
// answers T alone — the shape half of Go's standard library is written in, and
// the one the Go importer reads off a signature it can see. A declaration named
// here has no signature to read, so the mark is where it is said.
//
// It describes a call, so a struct refuses it.
// `method` says the identifier is invoked on its first argument rather than
// handed it, which is what a Go method wants: `c.Circle(1, 2)` rather than the
// method-expression spelling `canvas.Context.Circle(c, 1, 2)`. Both are valid;
// only the API knows which it is.
//
// `schedules` says the identifier runs a callback it is handed from the loop it
// owns, rather than inline on the thread that called it: `gtk4rt.Every` gives
// its function to a GLib timeout source, and the source runs it from the main
// loop, which is the thread the target draws on.
//
// passAsyncOffload reads it to know the callback is a body it may enter. A
// blocking call inside one is work that has to leave the drawing thread, and
// without the flag it stays there -- silently, which is the failure
// testdata/timer_tick_async_offload.txtar exists to deny.
//
// It is a flag rather than something read off the call, for the reason nothing
// is inferred from a foreign declaration at all: its SNGL body describes the
// identifier and does not implement it, so nothing in the program says when the
// host runs an argument.
// `context` says the Go function takes a leading context.Context that SNGL
// does not: the importer strips it from the signature and the call site
// supplies one. It is the same fact HasErrorReturn is for `fails` -- read off
// a signature by an importer, and unreadable here, so the mark states it.
enum NativeFlag { fails, method, schedules, context }

#[native("git.duckfam.us/jonathan/sngl/pkg/go/remote/http", "http.Get", fails)]
func httpGet(url string) http.Result

// What sngl:remote/http declares and leaves to each target. An override rather
// than an intrinsic: there is no string for a code generator to build here,
// only a call to name.
func http.get[language]() {
    return httpGet(url)
}

// --- Drawing ---
//
// `pkg/go/canvas.Context` is the raster surface every Go target that draws
// with pixels shares — fyne rasterises it into an image, bubbletea hands it to
// the terminal renderer. Its methods are the drawing vocabulary, so they are
// declared here rather than translated in Go: an override below calls them,
// and the emitted code is the call it wrote.
//
// On the *language* axis, so a platform inherits it by not overriding. gtk4
// and android do override, because cairo and Compose are not this surface.
// The runtime package the drawing surface lives in, named once: a mark
// argument may be a const, so the twenty declarations below do not each spell
// the import path.
const _canvasPkg = "git.duckfam.us/jonathan/sngl/pkg/go/canvas"

// The Go type of the draw handler's context parameter. Named through this
// package's own `native`, which is the point of registering a package's macros
// ahead of the marks that name them.
#[native(_canvasPkg, "*snglcanvas.Context")]
struct DrawContext {}

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.Save", method)]
func DrawContext.save()

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.Restore", method)]
func DrawContext.restore()

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.SetFill", method)]
func DrawContext.setFill(r uint8, g uint8, b uint8, a uint8)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.SetStroke", method)]
func DrawContext.setStroke(r uint8, g uint8, b uint8, a uint8)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.SetStrokeWidth", method)]
func DrawContext.setStrokeWidth(w float)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.SetFont", method)]
func DrawContext.setFont(size float, family string)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.SetLineStyle", method)]
func DrawContext.setLineStyle(cap string, join string)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.Circle", method)]
func DrawContext.circle(cx float, cy float, r float)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.Rect", method)]
func DrawContext.rect(x float, y float, w float, h float)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.Ellipse", method)]
func DrawContext.ellipse(cx float, cy float, rx float, ry float)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.Line", method)]
func DrawContext.line(x1 float, y1 float, x2 float, y2 float)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.MoveTo", method)]
func DrawContext.moveTo(x float, y float)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.LineTo", method)]
func DrawContext.lineTo(x float, y float)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.CubicTo", method)]
func DrawContext.cubicTo(x1 float, y1 float, x2 float, y2 float, x3 float, y3 float)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.ArcTo", method)]
func DrawContext.arcTo(x1 float, y1 float, x2 float, y2 float, r float)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.ClosePath", method)]
func DrawContext.closePath()

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.PaintPath", method)]
func DrawContext.paintPath()

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.Text", method)]
func DrawContext.text(x float, y float, content string)

#[native(_canvasPkg, "canvas.Context.Image", method)]
func DrawContext.image(x float, y float, w float, h float, src string)

// The primitive an override paints through. Its only job is to bind the
// context: `@draw` is the target calling in, and the handler body is what the
// draw function emits. Bodyless because the render is the handler's, not the
// declaration's.
// What a `@draw` handler is handed: the context to paint through, and the
// coordinate space the shapes were placed in.
struct DrawEvent {
    ctx DrawContext
    width int
    height int
}

#[marks.intrinsic("go:draw")]
component draw(@draw DrawEvent) shapes.shape

// The style setters, written once rather than per shape. The context keeps a
// pending style that the next primitive consumes, so this is the whole of
// "apply a style" and it is a plain call sequence an override can skip.
func applyStyle(ctx DrawContext, style shapes.CanvasStyle) {
    ctx.setFill(style.fill.r, style.fill.g, style.fill.b, style.fill.a)
    ctx.setStroke(style.stroke.r, style.stroke.g, style.stroke.b, style.stroke.a)
    ctx.setStrokeWidth(style.strokeWidth)
    ctx.setFont(style.fontSize, style.fontFamily)
    ctx.setLineStyle(style.lineCap, style.lineJoin)
}

// The shapes, on the language axis: every Go target that draws with pixels
// shares this surface, so fyne and bubbletea inherit these by not overriding
// them. gtk4 and android are `--lang go` too and do override -- cairo and
// Compose are their own -- and a platform override wins, which is what makes
// one implementation serve the two that want it.
//
// Each shape sets only the style it uses and gates each paint on its own
// alpha, so a fill-only shape does not stroke and a line does not read `fill`
// at all.

component shapes.rect[language] {
    draw(@draw(e) {
        e.ctx.save()
        applyStyle(e.ctx, style)
        e.ctx.rect(x, y, w, h)
        e.ctx.restore()
    })
}

component shapes.circle[language] {
    draw(@draw(e) {
        e.ctx.save()
        applyStyle(e.ctx, style)
        e.ctx.circle(cx, cy, r)
        e.ctx.restore()
    })
}

component shapes.ellipse[language] {
    draw(@draw(e) {
        e.ctx.save()
        applyStyle(e.ctx, style)
        e.ctx.ellipse(cx, cy, rx, ry)
        e.ctx.restore()
    })
}

component shapes.line[language] {
    draw(@draw(e) {
        e.ctx.save()
        applyStyle(e.ctx, style)
        e.ctx.line(x1, y1, x2, y2)
        e.ctx.restore()
    })
}

component shapes.path[language] {
    draw(@draw(e) {
        e.ctx.save()
        applyStyle(e.ctx, style)
        for var c = cmds {
            if c.op == "moveTo" {
                e.ctx.moveTo(c.x, c.y)
            }
            if c.op == "lineTo" {
                e.ctx.lineTo(c.x, c.y)
            }
            if c.op == "bezierTo" {
                e.ctx.cubicTo(c.cx1, c.cy1, c.cx2, c.cy2, c.x, c.y)
            }
            if c.op == "arcTo" {
                e.ctx.arcTo(c.cx1, c.cy1, c.x, c.y, c.r)
            }
            if c.op == "close" {
                e.ctx.closePath()
            }
        }
        e.ctx.paintPath()
        e.ctx.restore()
    })
}

component shapes.canvasText[language] {
    draw(@draw(e) {
        e.ctx.save()
        applyStyle(e.ctx, style)
        e.ctx.text(x, y, content)
        e.ctx.restore()
    })
}

component shapes.canvasImage[language] {
    draw(@draw(e) {
        e.ctx.save()
        e.ctx.image(x, y, w, h, src)
        e.ctx.restore()
    })
}

// --- Channels ---
//
// A host channel, named so a Go API that hands one out can be described at all:
// `*time.Ticker`'s `C` is a `<-chan Time`, and without this there is no type to
// declare that field as.
//
// **Direction is not spelled, and that is a gap rather than a decision.** A
// receive-only channel is reachable only because nothing writes a Go type for an
// expression that is merely selected on: `ticker.C` is passed straight to
// `go.recv` and no annotation is emitted for it. Bind one to a var and this type
// emits the bidirectional `chan T`, which Go will not assign a `<-chan T` to.
// Closing that means two types, or a direction on this one; nothing here needs
// it yet, and the first API that hands a channel out to be *stored* will.
#[marks.builtin("chan")]
struct chan<T> {}

// The element type comes from the argument rather than from a type argument,
// which a call site has no syntax for: `makeChan(false)` is a `chan<bool>`. The
// value itself is not used -- a channel has no initial contents -- so writing
// the zero of the type reads as what it is.
#[marks.intrinsic("go.makechan")]
func makeChan<T>(zero T) chan<T>

#[marks.intrinsic("go.close")]
func closeChan<T>(c chan<T>)

// One arm of a `select`: a receive from c, and what to run with the value.
//
// A value only so that `select` can be handed a list of them. Nothing holds one
// and nothing reads one but the emitter, which takes the channel and the body
// apart again to write the case.
struct Case {}

// Write one only inside the list a `select` is handed. Anywhere else the
// emitter has no case to put it in, and the failure is the positionless parse
// error described on `select`.
#[marks.intrinsic("go.recv")]
func recv<T>(c chan<T>, body func(T)) Case

// Blocks until one arm is ready and runs exactly that one. Real `select`
// syntax, which is what makes it a choice rather than a sequence of receives:
// two receives in a row would take the first channel's value before ever
// looking at the second.
//
// Two shapes behave in ways worth knowing, and both are Go's rather than this
// declaration's:
//
//   - A **closed** channel is always ready and yields the zero value, forever.
//     That is the idiom rather than the hazard: closing a `done` channel is how
//     a loop is told to stop, and its arm is chosen on the very next pass.
//   - A **nil** channel is never ready, so its arm is never chosen. The zero
//     value of `chan<T>` is nil, so a select over arms that were never made
//     blocks for the life of the process.
// The arms are written at the call, as a list literal: the emitter takes each
// one apart to write a case, so a list it cannot read is a list it cannot turn
// into arms.
//
// **Write them there, because the failure is a poor one.** Bind the arms to a
// var and the emitter declines, the call falls through to the ordinary path,
// and the Go that comes out says `m.select(arms)` -- `select` being a keyword,
// the build then fails in `go format` with a parse error and *no SNGL
// position*, having dumped the generated file. Same for a `recv` written
// anywhere but inside one of these lists.
//
// It stays a poor diagnostic because both *rule* shapes are wrong here: the
// checker knows nothing of a target's intrinsics by design, and a lowering pass
// cannot name this id without importing the plugin that declares it -- lower is
// imported by codegen, not the reverse.
//
// The emitter reporting a *position* is a different question and is **not**
// blocked. selectLines already holds the call's AST position, the same one
// async_capable.go reads, and the three Go platforms' Generate already return an
// error, so an additive errs field drained there is three call sites and no new
// interface. It is untaken rather than impossible: it buys this one callee a
// diagnostic while every other positionless emitter failure stays as it is,
// which is a choice worth making deliberately rather than here.
#[marks.intrinsic("go.select")]
func select(cases list<Case>)
